$ ============================================================================
$ HASHBOX v1.0 -- a public Fly SHA-256 library (bring hashbox)
$ ============================================================================
$
$ HASHBOX milestone. A genuine SHA-256 (FIPS 180-4) module: the two native
$ jobs below bind straight to libflyrt's OpenSSL-backed digest primitives
$ (runtime/hashbox.c, see flyrt.h's §5.8) -- there is NO hashbox special
$ case anywhere in fly-cc. `bring hashbox` resolves through the ordinary
$ public-module mechanism (docs/architecture.md §3.6 / driver.cpp's
$ resolveModule) exactly like sleep/http, and pulls in exactly this file's
$ jobs, callable spellings:
$
$   sha256(...)            flat name (the merged top-level namespace)
$   hashbox.sha256(...)    qualified name, at any `bring hashbox` site
$   hb.sha256(...)         module-alias spelling, at a `bring hashbox as hb`
$                          site (HASHBOX milestone aliasing -- see ast.h's
$                          Program::module_aliases and driver.cpp's
$                          ModuleMerger)
$
$ Public API:
$   hashbox.sha256(text)      -> tex: 64-char lowercase hex SHA-256 of the
$                               UTF-8 bytes of the tex argument
$   hashbox.file_sha256(path) -> tex: 64-char lowercase hex SHA-256 of a
$                               FILE's bytes. The runtime reads the file in
$                               64K chunks (never the whole file in memory),
$                               so hashing multi-hundred-MB artifacts (the
$                               release-checksums use case) is fine.
$
$ Deterministic lowercasing: unlike an "it depends on the tool" digest, the
$ hex output is always lowercase, so checksum files generated by this
$ module (e.g. "<digest>  <name>" lines) match the common lowercase line
$ format and can be diffed/verified byte-for-byte.
$
$ Errors: a non-tex argument, an unreadable/missing file, or an OpenSSL
$ failure throws a catchable Fly error -- handle with the usual
$ do { ... } grab (err) { ... } idiom, e.g.
$
$   do {
$     d = hashbox.file_sha256("missing.txt")
$   } grab (err) {
$     show("could not hash: {err}")
$   }

native job sha256(text)
native job file_sha256(path)